## Tech Stack

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Python | Main application language |
| Flask | Web dashboard and routes |
| Flask-WTF | Forms and CSRF protection |
| Syncthing | Device-to-device file synchronization |
| Tailscale Serve | Remote HTTPS access |
| Raspberry Pi | Optional storage or synchronization node |
| OpenCV and Pillow | Image conversion |
| PyMuPDF, pdf2docx, python-docx | PDF and document conversion |
| imageio-ffmpeg | Audio and video conversion |
| python-dotenv | Local environment configuration |

## Architecture

```text
Remote device with Tailscale
        │
        │ HTTPS connection
        ▼
Tailscale Serve
        │
        ▼
Flask dashboard
127.0.0.1:5000
        │
        ├── Browse and download files
        ├── Convert supported file types
        └── Syncthing API integration
                │
                ▼
       Syncthing shared storage folder
                │
                ▼
      Raspberry Pi or second Syncthing node
```

## Requirements

This project is currently developed and tested on Windows.

The host computer needs:

- Python 3
- Syncthing
- A configured Syncthing shared folder
- A Syncthing API key
- Tailscale for private remote HTTPS access
- Microsoft Word for DOCX-to-PDF conversion with `docx2pdf`
- Optional: a Raspberry Pi running Syncthing

Remote devices need:

- A modern web browser
- Tailscale installed and signed in with an authorized account

## Installation

### 1. Download the project

Clone the repository:

```powershell
git clone [https://github.com/YOUR-GITHUB-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY-NAME.git](https://github.com/YOUR-GITHUB-USERNAME/YOUR-REPOSITORY-NAME.git)
cd YOUR-REPOSITORY-NAME
```

Or download the repository as a ZIP file and extract it.

### 2. Create a virtual environment

```powershell
py -3 -m venv venv
```

### 3. Activate the virtual environment

```powershell
.\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

If PowerShell blocks the activation script, run this for the current PowerShell
window:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
.\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

### 4. Install dependencies

```powershell
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Configuration

Copy `.env.example` to a new `.env` file:

```powershell
Copy-Item .env.example .env
```

Edit `.env` and replace the placeholder values with your own local
configuration:

```env
SECRET_KEY=replace-with-a-long-random-secret
APP_USERNAME=replace-with-your-username
APP_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-strong-password

SYNCTHING_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8384
SYNCTHING_API_KEY=replace-with-your-syncthing-api-key

SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH=C:\path\to\your\syncthing-folder
```

Keep `.env` private. It contains passwords, API keys, and computer-specific
paths.

Do not commit `.env` to GitHub.

## Syncthing Setup

Syncthing performs the actual file synchronization. The Flask dashboard provides
the browser interface for those synchronized files.

1. Install Syncthing on the Windows host computer.
2. Create or select a shared folder in Syncthing.
3. Share that folder with a Raspberry Pi or another trusted device.
4. Copy the Syncthing API key into `.env`.
5. Set `SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH` in `.env` to the local path of the Syncthing shared
   folder.

Example:

```env
SYNCED_FOLDER_PATH=C:\Users\YourName\Syncthing
```

Do not expose Syncthing's GUI or API port publicly:

```text
8384
```

## Run the App

Activate the virtual environment:

```powershell
.\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Start the app:

```powershell
python app.py
```

Or run it directly without activation:

```powershell
.\venv\Scripts\python.exe app.py
```

Open the dashboard on the host computer:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:5000
```

To stop the app, return to PowerShell and press:

```text
Ctrl + C
```

## Remote HTTPS Access

This project uses Tailscale Serve for private remote HTTPS access.

Tailscale lets authorized devices access the dashboard without router
port-forwarding or exposing the Flask server directly to the public internet.

### Set up Tailscale

1. Install Tailscale on the computer hosting the Flask app.
2. Install Tailscale on every trusted phone, tablet, or computer that needs
   remote access.
3. Sign in with authorized Tailscale accounts.
4. Start the Flask app locally.
5. Open a second normal PowerShell window.
6. Run:

   ```powershell
   tailscale serve localhost:5000
   ```

7. Check the HTTPS route:

   ```powershell
   tailscale serve status
   ```

Tailscale displays an address similar to:

```text
[https://your-device-name.your-tailnet.ts.net](https://your-device-name.your-tailnet.ts.net)
```

Open that address from an authorized Tailscale-connected device.

The Flask application stays bound to:

```text
127.0.0.1:5000
```

Tailscale Serve securely routes HTTPS traffic to that local application.

### When the app is closed

The Tailscale connection can remain active while the Flask app is closed.

However, when `app.py` is not running, nothing is listening on local port
`5000`, so the Tailscale HTTPS address cannot load the dashboard.

This lets Tailscale continue supporting other trusted devices, such as a
Raspberry Pi Syncthing node, while the dashboard stays offline.

## Raspberry Pi Setup

A Raspberry Pi is optional, but it can act as a storage, backup, or Syncthing
synchronization node.

```text
Windows computer running the Flask dashboard
        │
        │ Syncthing file synchronization
        ▼
Raspberry Pi running Syncthing
```

To use a Raspberry Pi:

1. Install Syncthing on the Raspberry Pi.
2. Pair the Raspberry Pi with the Windows computer in Syncthing.
3. Share the same folder with both devices.
4. Wait for Syncthing to finish synchronization.
5. Optionally install Tailscale on the Raspberry Pi for secure remote
   connectivity.

The Raspberry Pi does not need `tailscale serve` unless it is hosting its own
web application.

## Security

This project includes several security-focused design choices:

- Flask runs with `debug=False`.
- The Flask app listens on `127.0.0.1` for Tailscale Serve access.
- Passwords, API keys, and secret keys are stored in `.env`.
- Forms use CSRF protection through Flask-WTF.
- Download and conversion routes validate requested paths.
- Path validation blocks `../` attempts outside the configured storage folder.
- Syncthing's GUI and API port `8384` are not exposed through the dashboard.
- Tailscale Serve provides private HTTPS access without router port forwarding.

Keep these rules:

- Do not publish `.env`.
- Do not publish passwords or API keys.
- Do not publish private Tailscale URLs.
- Do not publish personal files or screenshots containing personal documents.
- Do not port-forward Flask port `5000`.
- Do not port-forward Syncthing port `8384`.
- Do not use Tailscale Funnel for this project.

## File Conversion Notes

File conversion support depends on the programs and Python packages installed on
the host computer.

### Document conversion

DOCX-to-PDF conversion uses `docx2pdf`, which requires Microsoft Word on
Windows.

### Image conversion

Image conversion uses packages such as OpenCV and Pillow.

### PDF conversion

PDF and document conversion uses packages such as PyMuPDF, `pdf2docx`, and
`python-docx`.

### Audio and video conversion

Audio and video conversion uses `imageio-ffmpeg` and depends on available host
tools.

